.SH "DESCRIPTION"

.PP
 \fBgapplication\fR is a commandline implementation of the client\-side of the org\&.freedesktop\&.Application interface as specified by the freedesktop\&.org Desktop Entry Specification\&.

.PP
 \fBgapplication\fR can be used to start applications that have \fIDBusActivatable\fR set to true in their \fI\&.desktop\fR files and can be used to send messages to already\-running instances of other applications\&.

.PP
It is possible for applications to refer to \fBgapplication\fR in the \fIExec\fR line of their \fI\&.desktop\fR file to maintain backwards compatibility with implementations that do not directly support \fIDBusActivatable\fR\&.

.PP
 \fBgapplication\fR ships as part of GLib\&.

.SH "COMMANDS"

.SS "Global commands"

.TP
\fBhelp\fR [\fICOMMAND\fR]
Displays a short synopsis of the available commands or provides detailed help on a specific command\&.

.TP
\fBversion\fR
Prints the GLib version whence \fBgapplication\fR came\&.

.TP
\fBlist\-apps\fR
Prints a list of all application IDs that are known to support D\-Bus activation\&. This list is generated by scanning \fI\&.desktop\fR files as per the current \fBXDG_DATA_DIRS\fR\&.

.TP
\fBlaunch\fR \fIAPPID\fR [\fIFILE\fR...]
Launches an application\&.

The first parameter is the application ID in the familiar "reverse DNS" style (eg: 'org\&.gnome\&.app') without the \fI\&.desktop\fR suffix\&.

Optionally, if additional parameters are given, they are treated as the names of files to open and may be filenames or URIs\&. If no files are given then the application is simply activated\&.

.TP
\fBlist\-actions\fR \fIAPPID\fR
List the actions declared in the application's \fI\&.desktop\fR file\&. The parameter is the application ID, as above\&.

.TP
\fBaction\fR \fIAPPID\fR \fIACTION\fR [\fIPARAMETER\fR]
Invokes the named action (in the same way as would occur when activating an action specified in the \fI\&.desktop\fR file)\&.

The application ID (as above) is the first parameter\&. The action name follows\&.

Optionally, following the action name can be one parameter, in GVariant format, given as a single argument\&. Make sure to use sufficient quoting\&.

.SH "EXAMPLES"

.SS "From the commandline"

.PP
Launching an application:

.nf

        gapplication launch org\&.example\&.fooview
      
.fi

.PP
Opening a file with an application:

.nf

        gapplication launch org\&.example\&.fooview ~/file\&.foo
      
.fi

.PP
Opening many files with an application:

.nf

        gapplication launch org\&.example\&.fooview ~/foos/*\&.foo
      
.fi

.PP
Invoking an action on an application:

.nf

        gapplication action org\&.example\&.fooview create
      
.fi

.PP
Invoking an action on an application, with an action:

.nf

        gapplication action org\&.example\&.fooview show\-item '"item_id_828739"'
      
.fi

.SS "
        From the Exec lines of a .desktop file
      "

.PP
The commandline interface of \fBgapplication\fR was designed so that it could be used directly from the \fIExec\fR line of a \fI\&.desktop\fR file\&.

.PP
You might want to do this to allow for backwards compatibility with implementations of the specification that do not understand how to do D\-Bus activation, without having to install a separate utility program\&.

.PP
Consider the following example:

.nf

        [Desktop Entry]
        Version=1\&.1
        Type=Application
        Name=Foo Viewer
        DBusActivatable=true
        MimeType=image/x\-foo;
        Exec=gapplication launch org\&.example\&.fooview %F
        Actions=gallery;create;

        [Desktop Action gallery]
        Name=Browse Gallery
        Exec=gapplication action org\&.example\&.fooview gallery

        [Desktop Action create]
        Name=Create a new Foo!
        Exec=gapplication action org\&.example\&.fooview create
      
.fi

.SS "From a script"

.PP
If installing an application that supports D\-Bus activation you may still want to put a file in \fI/usr/bin\fR so that your program can be started from a terminal\&.

.PP
It is possible for this file to be a shell script\&. The script can handle arguments such as \-\-help and \-\-version directly\&. It can also parse other command line arguments and convert them to uses of \fBgapplication\fR to activate the application, open files, or invoke actions\&.

.PP
Here is a simplified example, as may be installed in \fI/usr/bin/fooview\fR:

.nf

        #!/bin/sh

        case "$1" in
          \-\-help)
            echo "see 'man fooview' for more information"
            ;;

          \-\-version)
            echo "fooview 1\&.2"
            ;;

          \-\-gallery)
            gapplication action org\&.example\&.fooview gallery
            ;;

          \-\-create)
            gapplication action org\&.example\&.fooview create
            ;;

          \-*)
            echo "unrecognised commandline argument"
            exit 1
            ;;

          *)
            gapplication launch org\&.example\&.fooview "$@"
            ;;
        esac
      
.fi

